{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-342263" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the Laplace transform $Y(s)=L{y(t)}$ if \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+y={u}_{2}(t)\\cdot {e}^{t-2}\\,,\\,y(0)=0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{2}(t)=u(t-2)$ is the unit step function.\n\n$Y(s)=$\n{{field-1}},$\\,s>1$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072958/state.json" "file_sha256" "d89a61e6c3c9cb584aeecb31908e15d576d3cabc0c5459a1b3f194dca39fdb34"} {"question" "q-342263" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides and use the addition and scalar multiplication properties of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{y}^{′}+y} & =L{{u}_{2}(t)\\cdot {e}^{t-2}} \\\\ L{{y}^{′}}+L{y} & =L{{u}_{2}(t)\\cdot {e}^{t-2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate $L{{y}^{′}},$ we use the formula for the Laplace transform of a derivative and the initial condition $y(0)=0:$\n\n$$\nL{{y}^{′}}=sY(s)-y(0)=sY(s)\n$$\n\nTo find the transform of the right-hand side, we note that the term \n\n$$\n{u}_{2}(t)\\cdot {e}^{t-2}\n$$\n\nis in the form $u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)$ where $a=2$ and $f(t)={e}^{t-2}.$ This can be interpreted as time-delayed exponential forcing. \n\nNow, recall that the second shifting theorem (time-shifting property) states\n\n$$\nL{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)}={e}^{-as}\\cdot L{f(t+a)}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nSo, by the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{u(t-2)\\cdot {e}^{t-2}} & ={e}^{-2s}\\cdot L{{e}^{(t+2)-2}} \\\\  & ={e}^{-2s}\\cdot L{{e}^{t}} \\\\  & ={e}^{-2s}\\cdot \\frac{1}{s-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-2s}}{s-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute these back into our transformed differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{y}^{′}}+L{y} & =L{{u}_{2}(t)\\cdot {e}^{t-2}} \\\\ sY(s)+Y(s) & =\\frac{{e}^{-2s}}{s-1} \\\\ (s+1)Y(s) & =\\frac{{e}^{-2s}}{s-1} \\\\ Y(s) & =\\frac{{e}^{-2s}}{(s-1)(s+1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072958/state.json" "file_sha256" "d89a61e6c3c9cb584aeecb31908e15d576d3cabc0c5459a1b3f194dca39fdb34"} {"question" "q-342263" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072958/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d695f8cbf8860c86c4dbdc734ebb89fde5f9238e4da3e192f23764d9b958362a"} {"question" "q-342263" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072958/state.json" "file_sha256" "d89a61e6c3c9cb584aeecb31908e15d576d3cabc0c5459a1b3f194dca39fdb34"} {"question" "q-342263" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{e^{-2s}}{(s-1)(s+1)}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072958/state.json" "file_sha256" "d89a61e6c3c9cb584aeecb31908e15d576d3cabc0c5459a1b3f194dca39fdb34"} {"question" "q-342772" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the Laplace transform $Y(s)=L{y(t)}$ if \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2y=5{u}_{\\pi /4}(t)\\cdot cos\\,(3t-\\frac{3\\pi }{4})\\,,\\,y(0)=-3,\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{\\pi /4}(t)=u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ is the unit step function.\n\nYou may make use of the following result:\n\n$$\nL{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\,s>0\n$$\n\n$Y(s)=-\\frac{3}{s+2}\\,+\\,$\n{{field-1}},$\\,s>0$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072958/state.json" "file_sha256" "d89a61e6c3c9cb584aeecb31908e15d576d3cabc0c5459a1b3f194dca39fdb34"} {"question" "q-342772" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides and use the addition and scalar multiplication properties of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{y}^{′}+2y} & =L{5{u}_{\\pi /4}(t)\\cdot cos\\,(3t-\\frac{3\\pi }{4})} \\\\ L{{y}^{′}}+2L{y} & =L{5{u}_{\\pi /4}(t)\\cdot cos\\,(3t-\\frac{3\\pi }{4})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate $L{{y}^{′}},$ we use the formula for the Laplace transform of a derivative and the initial condition $y(0)=-3:$\n\n$$\nL{{y}^{′}}=sY(s)-y(0)=sY(s)-(-3)=sY(s)+3\n$$\n\nTo find the transform of the right-hand side, we note that the term \n\n$$\n5{u}_{\\pi /4}(t)\\cdot cos\\,(3t-\\frac{3\\pi }{4})\n$$\n\nis in the form $u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)$ where $a=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ and $f(t)=5cos\\,(3t-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}).$ This can be interpreted as time-delayed sinusoidal forcing.\n\nNow, recall that the second shifting theorem (time-shifting property) states\n\n$$\nL{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)}={e}^{-as}\\cdot L{f(t+a)}.\n$$\n\nSo, by the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{5u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\cdot cos\\,(3t-\\frac{3\\pi }{4})} & ={e}^{-(\\pi /4)s}\\cdot L{5cos\\,(3\\,(t+\\frac{\\pi }{4})-\\frac{3\\pi }{4})} \\\\  & ={e}^{-\\pi s/4}\\cdot L{5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)} \\\\  & ={e}^{-\\pi s/4}\\cdot 5\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+9} \\\\  & =\\frac{5s{e}^{-\\pi s/4}}{{s}^{2}+9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute these back into our transformed differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{y}^{′}}+2L{y} & =L{5{u}_{\\pi /4}(t)cos\\,(3t-\\frac{3\\pi }{4})} \\\\ (sY(s)+3)+2Y(s) & =\\frac{5s{e}^{-\\pi s/4}}{{s}^{2}+9} \\\\ (s+2)Y(s)+3 & =\\frac{5s{e}^{-\\pi s/4}}{{s}^{2}+9} \\\\ (s+2)Y(s) & =-3+\\frac{5s{e}^{-\\pi s/4}}{{s}^{2}+9} \\\\ Y(s) & =-\\frac{3}{s+2}+\\frac{5s{e}^{-\\pi s/4}}{({s}^{2}+9)(s+2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072958/state.json" "file_sha256" "d89a61e6c3c9cb584aeecb31908e15d576d3cabc0c5459a1b3f194dca39fdb34"} {"question" "q-342772" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072958/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d695f8cbf8860c86c4dbdc734ebb89fde5f9238e4da3e192f23764d9b958362a"} {"question" "q-342772" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072958/state.json" "file_sha256" "d89a61e6c3c9cb584aeecb31908e15d576d3cabc0c5459a1b3f194dca39fdb34"} {"question" "q-342772" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{5se^{-\\pi s/4}}{(s^2+9)(s+2)}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072958/state.json" "file_sha256" "d89a61e6c3c9cb584aeecb31908e15d576d3cabc0c5459a1b3f194dca39fdb34"} {"question" "q-342532" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the Laplace transform $Y(s)=L{y(t)}$ if \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+4y=2{u}_{2}(t)\\cdot (t-2)^{3}\\,,\\,y(0)=0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{2}(t)=u(t-2)$ is the unit step function.\n\n$Y(s)=$\n{{field-1}},$\\,s>0$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072958/state.json" "file_sha256" "d89a61e6c3c9cb584aeecb31908e15d576d3cabc0c5459a1b3f194dca39fdb34"} {"question" "q-342532" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides and use the addition and scalar multiplication properties of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{y}^{′}+4y} & =L{2{u}_{2}(t)\\cdot (t-2)^{3}} \\\\ L{{y}^{′}}+4L{y} & =L{2{u}_{2}(t)\\cdot (t-2)^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate $L{{y}^{′}},$ we use the formula for the Laplace transform of a derivative and the initial condition $y(0)=0:$\n\n$$\nL{{y}^{′}}=sY(s)-y(0)=sY(s)\n$$\n\nTo find the transform of the right-hand side, we note that the term \n\n$$\n2{u}_{2}(t)\\cdot (t-2)^{3}\n$$\n\nis in the form $u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)$ where $a=2$ and $f(t)=2(t-2)^{3}.$\n\nNow, recall that the second shifting theorem (time-shifting property) states\n\n$$\nL{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)}={e}^{-as}\\cdot L{f(t+a)}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nSo, by the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{2u(t-2)\\cdot (t-2)^{3}} & ={e}^{-2s}\\cdot L{2((t+2)-2)^{3}} \\\\  & ={e}^{-2s}\\cdot L{2{t}^{3}} \\\\  & ={e}^{-2s}\\cdot 2\\cdot \\frac{3!}{{s}^{3+1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{12{e}^{-2s}}{{s}^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute these back into our transformed differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{y}^{′}}+4L{y} & =L{2{u}_{2}(t)(t-2)^{3}} \\\\ sY(s)+4Y(s) & =\\frac{12{e}^{-2s}}{{s}^{4}} \\\\ (s+4)Y(s) & =\\frac{12{e}^{-2s}}{{s}^{4}} \\\\ Y(s) & =\\frac{12{e}^{-2s}}{{s}^{4}(s+4)}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072958/state.json" "file_sha256" "d89a61e6c3c9cb584aeecb31908e15d576d3cabc0c5459a1b3f194dca39fdb34"} {"question" "q-342532" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072958/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d695f8cbf8860c86c4dbdc734ebb89fde5f9238e4da3e192f23764d9b958362a"} {"question" "q-342532" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072958/state.json" "file_sha256" "d89a61e6c3c9cb584aeecb31908e15d576d3cabc0c5459a1b3f194dca39fdb34"} {"question" "q-342532" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{12e^{-2s}}{s^4(s+4)}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072958/state.json" "file_sha256" "d89a61e6c3c9cb584aeecb31908e15d576d3cabc0c5459a1b3f194dca39fdb34"} {"question" "q-342540" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the Laplace transform $Y(s)=L{y(t)}$ if \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-2y=2{u}_{2}(t)\\cdot {e}^{4t-8}\\,,\\,y(0)=-1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{2}(t)=u(t-2)$ is the unit step function.\n\n$Y(s)=-\\frac{1}{s-2}\\,+\\,$\n{{field-1}},$\\,s>4$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072958/state.json" "file_sha256" "d89a61e6c3c9cb584aeecb31908e15d576d3cabc0c5459a1b3f194dca39fdb34"} {"question" "q-342540" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides and use the addition and scalar multiplication properties of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{y}^{′}-2y} & =L{2{u}_{2}(t)\\cdot {e}^{4t-8}} \\\\ L{{y}^{′}}-2L{y} & =L{2{u}_{2}(t)\\cdot {e}^{4t-8}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate $L{{y}^{′}},$ we use the formula for the Laplace transform of a derivative and the initial condition $y(0)=1:$\n\n$$\nL{{y}^{′}}=sY(s)-y(0)=sY(s)-(-1)=sY(s)+1\n$$\n\nTo find the transform of the right-hand side, we note that the term \n\n$$\n2{u}_{2}(t)\\cdot {e}^{4t-8}\n$$\n\nis in the form $u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)$ where $a=2$ and $f(t)=2{e}^{4t-8}.$\n\nNow, recall that the second shifting theorem (time-shifting property) states\n\n$$\nL{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)}={e}^{-as}\\cdot L{f(t+a)}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nSo, by the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{2u(t-2)\\cdot {e}^{4t-8}} & ={e}^{-2s}\\cdot L{2{e}^{4(t+2)-8}} \\\\  & ={e}^{-2s}\\cdot L{2{e}^{4t}} \\\\  & ={e}^{-2s}\\cdot \\frac{2}{s-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{e}^{-2s}}{s-4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute these back into our transformed differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{y}^{′}}-2L{y} & =L{2{u}_{2}(t){e}^{4t-8}} \\\\ (sY(s)+1)-2Y(s) & =\\frac{2{e}^{-2s}}{s-4} \\\\ (s-2)Y(s)+1 & =\\frac{2{e}^{-2s}}{s-4} \\\\ (s-2)Y(s) & =-1+\\frac{2{e}^{-2s}}{s-4} \\\\ Y(s) & =-\\frac{1}{s-2}+\\frac{2{e}^{-2s}}{(s-4)(s-2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072958/state.json" "file_sha256" "d89a61e6c3c9cb584aeecb31908e15d576d3cabc0c5459a1b3f194dca39fdb34"} {"question" "q-342540" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072958/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d695f8cbf8860c86c4dbdc734ebb89fde5f9238e4da3e192f23764d9b958362a"} {"question" "q-342540" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072958/state.json" "file_sha256" "d89a61e6c3c9cb584aeecb31908e15d576d3cabc0c5459a1b3f194dca39fdb34"} {"question" "q-342540" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{2e^{-2s}}{(s-4)(s-2)}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072958/state.json" "file_sha256" "d89a61e6c3c9cb584aeecb31908e15d576d3cabc0c5459a1b3f194dca39fdb34"} {"question" "q-342267" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the Laplace transform $Y(s)=L{y(t)}$ if \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+y={u}_{\\pi }(t)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-\\pi )\\,,\\,y(0)=0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{\\pi }(t)=u(t-\\pi )$ is the unit step function.\n\nYou may make use of the following result:\n\n$$\nL{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t}=\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\,s>0\n$$\n\n$Y(s)=$\n{{field-1}},$\\,s>0$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072958/state.json" "file_sha256" "d89a61e6c3c9cb584aeecb31908e15d576d3cabc0c5459a1b3f194dca39fdb34"} {"question" "q-342267" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides and use the addition and scalar multiplication properties of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{y}^{′}+y} & =L{{u}_{\\pi }(t)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-\\pi )} \\\\ L{{y}^{′}}+L{y} & =L{{u}_{\\pi }(t)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-\\pi )}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate $L{{y}^{′}},$ we use the formula for the Laplace transform of a derivative and the initial condition $y(0)=0:$\n\n$$\nL{{y}^{′}}=sY(s)-y(0)=sY(s)\n$$\n\nTo find the transform of the right-hand side, we note that the term \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\pi }(t)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-\\pi )\n$$\n\nis in the form $u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)$ where $a=\\pi $ and $f(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-\\pi ).$ This can be interpreted as time-delayed sinusoidal forcing. \n\nNow, recall that the second shifting theorem (time-shifting property) states\n\n$$\nL{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)}={e}^{-as}\\cdot L{f(t+a)}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nSo, by the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{u(t-\\pi )\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-\\pi )} & ={e}^{-\\pi s}\\cdot L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡((t+\\pi )-\\pi )} \\\\  & ={e}^{-\\pi s}\\cdot L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)} \\\\  & ={e}^{-\\pi s}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-\\pi s}}{{s}^{2}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute these back into our transformed differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{y}^{′}}+L{y} & =L{{u}_{\\pi }(t)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-\\pi )} \\\\ sY(s)+Y(s) & =\\frac{{e}^{-\\pi s}}{{s}^{2}+1} \\\\ (s+1)Y(s) & =\\frac{{e}^{-\\pi s}}{{s}^{2}+1} \\\\ Y(s) & =\\frac{{e}^{-\\pi s}}{({s}^{2}+1)(s+1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072958/state.json" "file_sha256" "d89a61e6c3c9cb584aeecb31908e15d576d3cabc0c5459a1b3f194dca39fdb34"} {"question" "q-342267" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072958/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d695f8cbf8860c86c4dbdc734ebb89fde5f9238e4da3e192f23764d9b958362a"} {"question" "q-342267" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072958/state.json" "file_sha256" "d89a61e6c3c9cb584aeecb31908e15d576d3cabc0c5459a1b3f194dca39fdb34"} {"question" "q-342267" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{e^{-\\pi s}}{(s^2+1)(s+1)}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072958/state.json" "file_sha256" "d89a61e6c3c9cb584aeecb31908e15d576d3cabc0c5459a1b3f194dca39fdb34"} {"question" "q-342812" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+3y={u}_{2}(t)\\cdot {e}^{-4(t-2)}\\,,\\,y(0)=0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{2}(t)=u(t-2)$ is the unit step function.\n\nYou're given that the Laplace transform $Y(s)=L{y(t)}$ is\n\n$$\nY(s)=\\frac{{e}^{-2s}}{(s+3)(s+4)}\n$$\n\nand that the solution to the initial value problem can be expressed as \n\n$$\ny(t)=u(t-2)\\cdot g(t).\n$$\n\nFind the function $g(t).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072958/state.json" "file_sha256" "d89a61e6c3c9cb584aeecb31908e15d576d3cabc0c5459a1b3f194dca39fdb34"} {"question" "q-342812" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To solve the initial value problem, we need to compute the inverse Laplace transform of $Y(s).$\n\nTo find the inverse transform, we first ignore the exponential term ${e}^{-2s}$ (which corresponds to a time shift) and focus on the rational function part:\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{1}{(s+3)(s+4)}.\n$$\n\nWe'll solve this in three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Partial fraction decomposition\n\nSince the denominator contains distinct linear factors, the decomposition takes the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{(s+3)(s+4)}=\\frac{A}{s+3}+\\frac{B}{s+4}.\n$$\n\nTo find the constants, we clear the denominators:\n\n$$\n1=A(s+4)+B(s+3).\n$$\n\nNow, we find the constants $A$ and $B.$\n\n- Setting $s=-3,$ we have\n\n$$\n1=A(1)+B(0)\\,⟹\\,A=1.\n$$\n\n- Setting $s=-4,$ we have\n\n$$\n1=A(0)+B(-1)\\,⟹\\,B=-1.\n$$\n\nThus, we can rewrite $F(s)$ as\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{1}{s+3}-\\frac{1}{s+4}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Finding the Inverse of the base function\n\nWe find the inverse transform $f(t)={L}^{-1}{F(s)}$ using standard transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & ={L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s+3}-\\frac{1}{s+4}} \\\\  & ={e}^{-3t}-{e}^{-4t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Applying the time-shifting property\n\nFinally, we address the ${e}^{-2s}$ term. By the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n{e}^{-as}\\cdot F(s)=L{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a)}.\n$$\n\nTaking the inverse Laplace transform gives\n\n$$\n{L}^{-1}{{e}^{-as}F(s)}=u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=2.$ Therefore, we shift $f(t)$ by replacing every instance of $t$ with $(t-2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t-2)f(t-2) & =u(t-2)[{e}^{-3(t-2)}-{e}^{-4(t-2)}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing this to the form given in the problem statement $y(t)=u(t-2)\\cdot g(t),$ we identify $g(t)$ as\n\n$$\ng(t)={e}^{-3(t-2)}-{e}^{-4(t-2)}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072958/state.json" "file_sha256" "d89a61e6c3c9cb584aeecb31908e15d576d3cabc0c5459a1b3f194dca39fdb34"} {"question" "q-342812" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072958/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d695f8cbf8860c86c4dbdc734ebb89fde5f9238e4da3e192f23764d9b958362a"} {"question" "q-342812" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072958/state.json" "file_sha256" "d89a61e6c3c9cb584aeecb31908e15d576d3cabc0c5459a1b3f194dca39fdb34"} {"question" "q-342812" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-{e}^{-3(t-2)}+{e}^{-4(t-2)}"] ["math" "2{e}^{-3(t-2)}-{e}^{-4(t-2)}"] ["math" "{e}^{-3(t-2)}+{e}^{-4(t-2)}"] ["math" "{e}^{-3(t-2)}-{e}^{-4(t-2)}"] ["math" "{e}^{-3t}-{e}^{-4t}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072958/state.json" "file_sha256" "d89a61e6c3c9cb584aeecb31908e15d576d3cabc0c5459a1b3f194dca39fdb34"} {"question" "q-342812" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{e}^{-3(t-2)}-{e}^{-4(t-2)}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072958/state.json" "file_sha256" "d89a61e6c3c9cb584aeecb31908e15d576d3cabc0c5459a1b3f194dca39fdb34"} {"question" "q-342314" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+y={u}_{2}(t)\\cdot (t-2)\\,,\\,y(0)=1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{2}(t)=u(t-2)$ is the unit step function.\n\nYou're given that the Laplace transform $Y(s)=L{y(t)}$ is\n\n$$\nY(s)=\\frac{1}{s+1}+\\frac{{e}^{-2s}}{{s}^{2}(s+1)}\n$$\n\nand that the solution to the initial value problem can be expressed as \n\n$$\ny(t)={e}^{-t}+u(t-2)\\cdot g(t).\n$$\n\nFind the function $g(t).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072958/state.json" "file_sha256" "d89a61e6c3c9cb584aeecb31908e15d576d3cabc0c5459a1b3f194dca39fdb34"} {"question" "q-342314" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the function $g(t),$ we need to compute the inverse Laplace transform of the term containing the time delay in $Y(s).$\n\nWe focus on the term with the exponential ${e}^{-2s}$ (which corresponds to the step function in the time domain) and identify the rational function part:\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}(s+1)}\n$$\n\nWe'll solve this in three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Partial fraction decomposition\n\nSince the denominator contains a linear factor $(s+1)$ and a repeated linear factor ${s}^{2},$ the decomposition takes the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}(s+1)}=\\frac{A}{s}+\\frac{B}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{C}{s+1}.\n$$\n\nTo find the constants, we clear the denominators:\n\n$$\n1=As(s+1)+B(s+1)+C{s}^{2}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the constants $A,B,$ and $C.$\n\n- Setting $s=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n1=A(0)+B(1)+C(0)\\,⟹\\,B=1.\n$$\n\n- Setting $s=-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n1=A(0)+B(0)+C(1)\\,⟹\\,C=1.\n$$\n\n- Comparing the coefficients of ${s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n0=A+C\\,⟹\\,A=-C=-1.\n$$\n\nThus, we can rewrite $F(s)$ as\n\n$$\nF(s)=-\\frac{1}{s}+\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{s+1}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Finding the Inverse of the base function\n\nWe find the inverse transform $f(t)={L}^{-1}{F(s)}$ using standard transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & ={L}^{-1}{-\\frac{1}{s}+\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{s+1}} \\\\  & =-1+t+{e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Applying the time-shifting property\n\nFinally, we address the ${e}^{-2s}$ term. By the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n{e}^{-as}\\cdot F(s)=L{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a)}.\n$$\n\nTaking the inverse Laplace transform gives\n\n$$\n{L}^{-1}{{e}^{-as}F(s)}=u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=2.$ Therefore, we shift $f(t)$ by replacing every instance of $t$ with $(t-2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t-2)f(t-2) & =u(t-2)(-1+(t-2)+{e}^{-(t-2)}) \\\\  & =u(t-2)(t-3+{e}^{-(t-2)})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing this to the second term of the form given in the problem statement $y(t)={e}^{-t}+u(t-2)\\cdot g(t),$ we identify $g(t)$ as\n\n$$\ng(t)={e}^{-(t-2)}+t-3.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072958/state.json" "file_sha256" "d89a61e6c3c9cb584aeecb31908e15d576d3cabc0c5459a1b3f194dca39fdb34"} {"question" "q-342314" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072958/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d695f8cbf8860c86c4dbdc734ebb89fde5f9238e4da3e192f23764d9b958362a"} {"question" "q-342314" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072958/state.json" "file_sha256" "d89a61e6c3c9cb584aeecb31908e15d576d3cabc0c5459a1b3f194dca39fdb34"} {"question" "q-342314" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{e}^{-(t-2)}+t-1"] ["math" "{e}^{-(t-2)}+t-2"] ["math" "{e}^{-(t-2)}+t-3"] ["math" "{e}^{-(t-2)}-t+3"] ["math" "{e}^{-(t-2)}-t-1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072958/state.json" "file_sha256" "d89a61e6c3c9cb584aeecb31908e15d576d3cabc0c5459a1b3f194dca39fdb34"} {"question" "q-342314" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{e}^{-(t-2)}+t-3"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072958/state.json" "file_sha256" "d89a61e6c3c9cb584aeecb31908e15d576d3cabc0c5459a1b3f194dca39fdb34"} {"question" "q-342815" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2y={u}_{\\pi /2}(t)\\cdot cos\\,(2t-\\pi )\\,,\\,y(0)=-2,\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{\\pi /2}(t)=u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ is the unit step function.\n\nYou're given that the Laplace transform $Y(s)=L{y(t)}$ is\n\n$$\nY(s)=-\\frac{2}{s+2}+\\frac{s\\,{e}^{-\\pi s/2}}{({s}^{2}+4)(s+2)}\n$$\n\nand that the solution to the initial value problem can be expressed as \n\n$$\ny(t)=-2{e}^{-2t}-\\frac{1}{4}u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cdot g(t).\n$$\n\nFind the function $g(t).$ \n\nHint: You may make use of the following results:\n\n$$\nL{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t}=\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\,L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\,s>0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072958/state.json" "file_sha256" "d89a61e6c3c9cb584aeecb31908e15d576d3cabc0c5459a1b3f194dca39fdb34"} {"question" "q-342815" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the function $g(t),$ we need to compute the inverse Laplace transform of the term containing the time delay in $Y(s).$\n\nWe focus on the term with the exponential ${e}^{-\\pi s/2}$ (which corresponds to the step function in the time domain) and identify the rational function part:\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{s}{({s}^{2}+4)(s+2)}.\n$$\n\nWe'll solve this in three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Partial fraction decomposition\n\nSince the denominator contains a linear factor $(s+2)$ and an irreducible quadratic factor $({s}^{2}+4),$ the decomposition takes the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{s}{({s}^{2}+4)(s+2)}=\\frac{A}{s+2}+\\frac{Bs+C}{{s}^{2}+4}.\n$$\n\nTo find the constants, we clear the denominators:\n\n$$\ns=A({s}^{2}+4)+(Bs+C)(s+2).\n$$\n\nNow, we find the constants $A,B,$ and $C.$\n\n- Setting $s=-2,$ we have\n\n$$\n-2=A((-2)^{2}+4)+0\\,⟹\\,8A=-2\\,⟹\\,A=-\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\n- Comparing the coefficients of ${s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n0=A+B\\,⟹\\,B=-A=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\n- Comparing the constant terms, we have\n\n$$\n0=4A+2C\\,⟹\\,0=4\\,(-\\frac{1}{4})+2C\\,⟹\\,C=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nThus, we can rewrite $F(s)$ as\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{1}{4}(-\\frac{1}{s+2}+\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+4}+\\frac{2}{{s}^{2}+4}).\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Finding the Inverse of the base function\n\nWe find the inverse transform $f(t)={L}^{-1}{F(s)}$ using standard transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =\\frac{1}{4}(-{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s+2}}+{L}^{-1}{\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+4}}+{L}^{-1}{\\frac{2}{{s}^{2}+4}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(-{e}^{-2t}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Applying the time-shifting property\n\nFinally, we address the ${e}^{-\\pi s/2}$ term. By the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n{e}^{-as}\\cdot F(s)=L{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a)}.\n$$\n\nTaking the inverse Laplace transform gives\n\n$$\n{L}^{-1}{{e}^{-as}F(s)}=u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Therefore, we shift $f(t)$ by replacing every instance of $t$ with $(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2})f\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}[-{e}^{-2(t-\\pi /2)}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2}))+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2}))].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the trigonometric identities $cos\\,(2\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2}))=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)$ and $sin\\,(2\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2}))=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),$ we simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}(-{e}^{-2(t-\\pi /2)}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\,u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2})({e}^{-2(t-\\pi /2)}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing this to the second term of the form given in the problem statement $y(t)=-2{e}^{-2t}-\\frac{1}{4}u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cdot g(t),$ we identify $g(t)$ as\n\n$$\ng(t)={e}^{-2(t-\\pi /2)}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072958/state.json" "file_sha256" "d89a61e6c3c9cb584aeecb31908e15d576d3cabc0c5459a1b3f194dca39fdb34"} {"question" "q-342815" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072958/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d695f8cbf8860c86c4dbdc734ebb89fde5f9238e4da3e192f23764d9b958362a"} {"question" "q-342815" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072958/state.json" "file_sha256" "d89a61e6c3c9cb584aeecb31908e15d576d3cabc0c5459a1b3f194dca39fdb34"} {"question" "q-342815" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-{e}^{-2(t-\\pi /2)}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)"] ["math" "-{e}^{-2(t-\\pi /2)}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)"] ["math" "{e}^{-2(t-\\pi /2)}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)"] ["math" "{e}^{-2(t-\\pi /2)}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)"] ["math" "{e}^{-2(t-\\pi /2)}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072958/state.json" "file_sha256" "d89a61e6c3c9cb584aeecb31908e15d576d3cabc0c5459a1b3f194dca39fdb34"} {"question" "q-342815" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{e}^{-2(t-\\pi /2)}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072958/state.json" "file_sha256" "d89a61e6c3c9cb584aeecb31908e15d576d3cabc0c5459a1b3f194dca39fdb34"} {"question" "q-342780" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+3y={u}_{3}(t)\\cdot (t-3)\\,,\\,y(0)=-2,\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{3}(t)=u(t-3)$ is the unit step function.\n\nYou're given that the Laplace transform $Y(s)=L{y(t)}$ is\n\n$$\nY(s)=-\\frac{2}{s+3}+\\frac{{e}^{-3s}}{{s}^{2}(s+3)}\n$$\n\nand that the solution to the initial value problem can be expressed as \n\n$$\ny(t)=-2{e}^{-3t}+u(t-3)\\cdot g(t).\n$$\n\nFind the function $g(t).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072958/state.json" "file_sha256" "d89a61e6c3c9cb584aeecb31908e15d576d3cabc0c5459a1b3f194dca39fdb34"} {"question" "q-342780" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the function $g(t),$ we need to compute the inverse Laplace transform of the term containing the time delay in $Y(s).$\n\nWe focus on the term with the exponential ${e}^{-3s}$ (which corresponds to the step function in the time domain) and identify the rational function part:\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}(s+3)}.\n$$\n\nWe'll solve this in three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Partial fraction decomposition\n\nSince the denominator contains a linear factor $(s+3)$ and a repeated linear factor ${s}^{2},$ the decomposition takes the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}(s+3)}=\\frac{A}{s}+\\frac{B}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{C}{s+3}.\n$$\n\nTo find the constants, we clear the denominators:\n\n$$\n1=As(s+3)+B(s+3)+C{s}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the constants $A,B,$ and $C.$\n\n- Setting $s=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n1=A(0)+B(3)+C(0)\\,⟹\\,B=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\n- Setting $s=-3,$ we have\n\n$$\n1=A(0)+B(0)+C(9)\\,⟹\\,C=\\frac{1}{9}.\n$$\n\n- Comparing the coefficients of ${s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n0=A+C\\,⟹\\,A=-C=-\\frac{1}{9}.\n$$\n\nThus, we can rewrite $F(s)$ as\n\n$$\nF(s)=-\\frac{1}{9s}+\\frac{1}{3{s}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{9(s+3)}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Finding the Inverse of the base function\n\nWe find the inverse transform $f(t)={L}^{-1}{F(s)}$ using standard transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & ={L}^{-1}{-\\frac{1}{9s}+\\frac{1}{3{s}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{9(s+3)}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{9}+\\frac{t}{3}+\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{-3t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Applying the time-shifting property\n\nFinally, we address the ${e}^{-3s}$ term. By the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n{e}^{-as}\\cdot F(s)=L{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a)}.\n$$\n\nTaking the inverse Laplace transform gives\n\n$$\n{L}^{-1}{{e}^{-as}F(s)}=u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=3.$ Therefore, we shift $f(t)$ by replacing every instance of $t$ with $(t-3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t-3)f(t-3) & =u(t-3)(-\\frac{1}{9}+\\frac{t-3}{3}+\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{-3(t-3)}) \\\\  & =u(t-3)(\\frac{3t-10}{9}+\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{-3(t-3)}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing this to the second term of the form given in the problem statement $y(t)=-2{e}^{-3t}+u(t-3)\\cdot g(t),$ we identify $g(t)$ as\n\n$$\ng(t)=\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{-3(t-3)}+\\frac{3t-10}{9}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072958/state.json" "file_sha256" "d89a61e6c3c9cb584aeecb31908e15d576d3cabc0c5459a1b3f194dca39fdb34"} {"question" "q-342780" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_captu