expressed as \n\n$$\ny(t)=2{e}^{t}+\\frac{1}{3}u(t-3)\\cdot g(t).\n$$\n\nFind the function $g(t).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072958/state.json" "file_sha256" "d89a61e6c3c9cb584aeecb31908e15d576d3cabc0c5459a1b3f194dca39fdb34"} {"question" "q-342814" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the function $g(t),$ we need to compute the inverse Laplace transform of the term containing the time delay in $Y(s).$\n\nWe focus on the term with the exponential ${e}^{-3s}$ (which corresponds to the step function in the time domain) and identify the rational function part:\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{1}{(s-1)(s+2)}.\n$$\n\nWe'll solve this in three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Partial fraction decomposition\n\nSince the denominator contains distinct linear factors, the decomposition takes the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{(s-1)(s+2)}=\\frac{A}{s-1}+\\frac{B}{s+2}.\n$$\n\nTo find the constants, we clear the denominators:\n\n$$\n1=A(s+2)+B(s-1).\n$$\n\nNow, we find the constants $A$ and $B.$\n\n- Setting $s=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n1=A(3)+B(0)\\,⟹\\,A=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\n- Setting $s=-2,$ we have\n\n$$\n1=A(0)+B(-3)\\,⟹\\,B=-\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nThus, we can rewrite $F(s)$ as\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{1}{3}(\\frac{1}{s-1}-\\frac{1}{s+2}).\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Finding the Inverse of the base function\n\nWe find the inverse transform $f(t)={L}^{-1}{F(s)}$ using standard transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =\\frac{1}{3}({L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s-1}}-{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s+2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}({e}^{t}-{e}^{-2t}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Applying the time-shifting property\n\nFinally, we address the ${e}^{-3s}$ term. By the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n{e}^{-as}\\cdot F(s)=L{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a)}.\n$$\n\nTaking the inverse Laplace transform gives\n\n$$\n{L}^{-1}{{e}^{-as}F(s)}=u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=3.$ Therefore, we shift $f(t)$ by replacing every instance of $t$ with $(t-3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t-3)f(t-3) & =\\frac{1}{3}u(t-3)\\cdot [{e}^{(t-3)}-{e}^{-2(t-3)}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing this to the second term of the form given in the problem statement $y(t)=2{e}^{t}+\\frac{1}{3}u(t-3)\\cdot g(t),$ we identify $g(t)$ as\n\n$$\ng(t)={e}^{(t-3)}-{e}^{-2(t-3)}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072958/state.json" "file_sha256" "d89a61e6c3c9cb584aeecb31908e15d576d3cabc0c5459a1b3f194dca39fdb34"} {"question" "q-342814" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072958/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d695f8cbf8860c86c4dbdc734ebb89fde5f9238e4da3e192f23764d9b958362a"} {"question" "q-342814" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072958/state.json" "file_sha256" "d89a61e6c3c9cb584aeecb31908e15d576d3cabc0c5459a1b3f194dca39fdb34"} {"question" "q-342814" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-{e}^{(t-3)}+{e}^{-2(t-3)}"] ["math" "{e}^{(t-3)}+{e}^{-2(t-3)}"] ["math" "{e}^{(t-3)}-{e}^{-2(t+3)}"] ["math" "{e}^{(t-3)}-{e}^{-2(t-3)}"] ["math" "{e}^{t}-{e}^{-2t}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072958/state.json" "file_sha256" "d89a61e6c3c9cb584aeecb31908e15d576d3cabc0c5459a1b3f194dca39fdb34"} {"question" "q-342814" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{e}^{(t-3)}-{e}^{-2(t-3)}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072958/state.json" "file_sha256" "d89a61e6c3c9cb584aeecb31908e15d576d3cabc0c5459a1b3f194dca39fdb34"} {"question" "e-21860" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the Laplace transform $Y(s) = \\mathcal L\\{y(t)\\}$ if \n\n$$\ny' - 3y = 2u_{\\pi/2}(t)\\cdot \\sin\\!\\left(2t-\\pi\\right)\\,, \\qquad y(0)=-2,\n$$\n\nwhere $u_{\\pi/2}(t)=u\\!\\left(t-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$ is the unit step function.\n\nYou may make use of the following result:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\{\\sin\\omega t\\} = \\dfrac{\\omega}{s^2+\\omega^2}, \\qquad s > 0\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072958/example-21860.json" "file_sha256" "28eb8ef01124e428171c6c837430b57bd4609ea3e609b2d56a3764b6d394cdc1"} {"question" "e-21860" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides and use the addition and scalar multiplication properties of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{y}^{′}-3y} & =L{2{u}_{\\pi /2}(t)\\cdot sin\\,(2t-\\pi )} \\\\ L{{y}^{′}}-3L{y} & =L{2{u}_{\\pi /2}(t)\\cdot sin\\,(2t-\\pi )}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate $\\mathcal L\\left\\{y' \\right\\},$ we use the formula for the Laplace transform of a derivative and the initial condition $y(0)=-2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{y' \\right\\}  = sY(s) - y(0)   = sY(s) + 2\n$$\n\nTo find the transform of the right-hand side, we note that the term \n\n$$\n2u_{\\pi/2}(t)\\cdot \\sin\\!\\left(2t-\\pi\\right)\n$$\n\nis in the form $u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)$ where $a=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$ and $f(t)=2\\sin(2t-\\pi).$ This can be interpreted as time-delayed sinusoidal forcing. \n\nNow, recall that the second shifting theorem (time-shifting property) states\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)\\} = e^{-as} \\cdot \\mathcal L\\{f(t+a)\\}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nSo, by the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cdot 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t-\\pi )} & ={e}^{-(\\pi /2)s}\\cdot L{2sin\\,(2\\,(t+\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\pi )} \\\\  & ={e}^{-(\\pi /2)s}\\cdot L{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)} \\\\  & ={e}^{-(\\pi /2)s}\\cdot 2\\cdot \\frac{2}{{s}^{2}+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{4{e}^{-\\pi s/2}}{{s}^{2}+4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute these back into our transformed differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{y}^{′}}-3L{y} & =L{2{u}_{\\pi /2}(t)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t-\\pi )} \\\\ (sY(s)+2)-3Y(s) & =\\frac{4{e}^{-\\pi s/2}}{{s}^{2}+4} \\\\ (s-3)Y(s)+2 & =\\frac{4{e}^{-\\pi s/2}}{{s}^{2}+4} \\\\ (s-3)Y(s) & =-2+\\frac{4{e}^{-\\pi s/2}}{{s}^{2}+4} \\\\ Y(s) & =-\\frac{2}{s-3}+\\frac{4{e}^{-\\pi s/2}}{({s}^{2}+4)(s-3)}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072958/example-21860.json" "file_sha256" "28eb8ef01124e428171c6c837430b57bd4609ea3e609b2d56a3764b6d394cdc1"} {"question" "e-21861" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' + 4y = u_3(t)\\cdot (t-3)\\,, \\qquad y(0)=-2,\n$$\n\nwhere $u_3(t)=u(t-3)$ is the unit step function.\n\nYou're given that the Laplace transform $Y(s) = \\mathcal L\\{y(t)\\}$ is\n\n$$\nY(s) = -\\dfrac{2}{s+4} + \\dfrac{e^{-3s}}{s^2(s+4)}\n$$\n\nand that the solution to the initial value problem can be expressed as \n\n$$\ny(t) = -2e^{-4t} + u(t-3)\\cdot g(t).\n$$\n\nFind the function $g(t).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072958/example-21861.json" "file_sha256" "8df8ee80e94ffd826996c5bc441f49eb836e6a3ce5e364a83ad65843e2304419"} {"question" "e-21861" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the function $g(t),$ we need to compute the inverse Laplace transform of the term containing the time delay in $Y(s).$\n\nWe focus on the term with the exponential $e^{-3s}$ (which corresponds to the step function in the time domain) and identify the rational function part:\n\n$$\nF(s) = \\dfrac{1}{s^2(s+4)}.\n$$\n\nWe'll solve this in three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Partial fraction decomposition\n\nSince the denominator contains a linear factor $(s+4)$ and a repeated linear factor $s^2,$ the decomposition takes the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{s^2(s+4)} = \\dfrac{A}{s} + \\dfrac{B}{s^2} + \\dfrac{C}{s+4}.\n$$\n\nTo find the constants, we clear the denominators:\n\n$$\n1 = As(s+4) + B(s+4) + Cs^2.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the constants $A, B,$ and $C.$\n\n- Setting $s=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n1 = A(0) + B(4) + C(0) \\implies B = \\dfrac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\n- Setting $s=-4,$ we have\n\n$$\n1 = A(0) + B(0) + C(16) \\implies C = \\dfrac{1}{16}.\n$$\n\n- Comparing the coefficients of $s^2,$ we have\n\n$$\n0 = A + C \\implies A = -C = -\\dfrac{1}{16}.\n$$\n\nThus, we can rewrite $F(s)$ as\n\n$$\nF(s) = -\\dfrac{1}{16s} + \\dfrac{1}{4s^2} + \\dfrac{1}{16(s+4)}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Finding the Inverse of the base function\n\nWe find the inverse transform $f(t) = \\mathcal L^{-1}\\{F(s)\\}$ using standard transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & ={L}^{-1}{-\\frac{1}{16s}+\\frac{1}{4{s}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{16(s+4)}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{16}+\\frac{t}{4}+\\frac{1}{16}{e}^{-4t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Applying the time-shifting property\n\nFinally, we address the $e^{-3s}$ term. By the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\ne^{-as}\\cdot F(s)  = \\mathcal L\\{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a)\\}.\n$$\n\nTaking the inverse Laplace transform gives\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L^{-1} \\{ e^{-as}F(s) \\} = u(t-a) \\cdot f(t-a).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=3.$ Therefore, we shift $f(t)$ by replacing every instance of $t$ with $(t-3){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t-3)f(t-3) & =u(t-3)[-\\frac{1}{16}+\\frac{t-3}{4}+\\frac{1}{16}{e}^{-4(t-3)}] \\\\  & =u(t-3)(\\frac{1}{16}{e}^{-4(t-3)}+\\frac{4t-13}{16}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing this to the second term of the form given in the problem statement $y(t) = -2e^{-4t} + u(t-3)\\cdot g(t),$ we identify $g(t)$ as\n\n$$\ng(t) = \\dfrac{1}{16}e^{-4(t-3)} + \\dfrac{4t-13}{16}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072958/example-21861.json" "file_sha256" "8df8ee80e94ffd826996c5bc441f49eb836e6a3ce5e364a83ad65843e2304419"}] "usage" {"e-21861" [] "q-342314" [] "e-21860" [] "q-342263" []} "basis" 2681 "retractions" [[17592186229692 :question/problem "Find the Laplace transform $Y(s) = L{y(t)}$ if\n$y^{′} + y = u_{2}(t) \\cdot e^{t - 2}, y(0) = 0$,\nwhere $u_{2}(t) = u(t - 2)$ is the unit step function.$Y(s) =$\n{{field-1}},$s > 1$"] [17592186229694 :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$y^{′} + y = u_{2}(t) \\cdot (t - 2), y(0) = 1$,\nwhere $u_{2}(t) = u(t - 2)$ is the unit step function.\n\nYou're given that the Laplace transform $Y(s) = L{y(t)}$ is\n$Y(s) = \\frac{1}{s + 1} + (e^{-2s})/(s^{2}(s + 1))$\nand that the solution to the initial value problem can be expressed as\n$y(t) = e^{-t} + u(t - 2) \\cdot g(t)$.\nFind the function $g(t)$."] [17592186228897 :question/problem "Find the Laplace transform $Y(s) = \\mathcal L\\{y(t)\\}$ if\n$y' - 3y = 2u_{\\pi/2}(t)\\cdot \\sin\\!\\left(2t-\\pi\\right)\\,, \\qquad y(0)=-2$,\nwhere $u_{\\pi/2}(t)=u\\!\\left(t-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$ is the unit step function.\n\nYou may make use of the following result:\n\n$\\mathcal L\\{\\sin\\omega t\\} = \\dfrac{\\omega}{s^2+\\omega^2}, \\qquad s > 0$"] [17592186228897 :question/worked-solution "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides and use the addition and scalar multiplication properties of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL{y^{′} - 3y} &= L{2u_{π/2}(t) \\cdot \\sin(2t - π)} \\\\\nL{y^{′}} - 3L{y} &= L{2u_{π/2}(t) \\cdot \\sin(2t - π)}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\left\\{y' \\right\\}\n$$\n\nwe use the formula for the Laplace transform of a derivative and the initial condition $y(0)=-2$:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{y' \\right\\} = sY(s) - y(0) = sY(s) + 2\n$$\n\nTo find the transform of the right-hand side, we note that the term\n\n$$\n2u_{\\pi/2}(t)\\cdot \\sin\\!\\left(2t-\\pi\\right)\n$$\n\nis in the form $u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)$ where\n\n$$\na=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nf(t)=2\\sin(2t-\\pi)\n$$\n\nThis can be interpreted as time-delayed sinusoidal forcing.\n\nNow, recall that the second shifting theorem (time-shifting property) states\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)\\} = e^{-as} \\cdot \\mathcal L\\{f(t+a)\\}\n$$\n\nSo, by the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL{u(t - \\frac{π}{2}) \\cdot 2\\sin (2t - π)} &= e^{- (π/2)s} \\cdot L{2\\sin(2(t + \\frac{π}{2}) - π)} \\\\\n&= e^{- (π/2)s} \\cdot L{2\\sin (2t)} \\\\\n&= e^{- (π/2)s} \\cdot 2 \\cdot \\frac{2}{s^{2} + 4} \\\\\n&= \\frac{4e^{- πs/2}}{s^{2} + 4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute these back into our transformed differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL{y^{′}} - 3L{y} &= L{2u_{π/2}(t)\\sin (2t - π)} \\\\\n(sY(s) + 2) - 3Y(s) &= \\frac{4e^{- πs/2}}{s^{2} + 4} \\\\\n(s - 3)Y(s) + 2 &= \\frac{4e^{- πs/2}}{s^{2} + 4} \\\\\n(s - 3)Y(s) &= -2 + \\frac{4e^{- πs/2}}{s^{2} + 4} \\\\\nY(s) &= -\\frac{2}{s - 3} + (4e^{- πs/2})/((s^{2} + 4)(s - 3))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186228898 :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$y' + 4y = u_3(t)\\cdot (t-3)\\,, \\qquad y(0)=-2$,\nwhere $u_3(t)=u(t-3)$ is the unit step function.\n\nYou're given that the Laplace transform $Y(s) = \\mathcal L\\{y(t)\\}$ is\n$Y(s) = -\\dfrac{2}{s+4} + \\dfrac{e^{-3s}}{s^2(s+4)}$\nand that the solution to the initial value problem can be expressed as\n$y(t) = -2e^{-4t} + u(t-3)\\cdot g(t)$.\nFind the function $g(t)$."] [17592186228898 :question/worked-solution "To find the function $g(t)$, we need to compute the inverse Laplace transform of the term containing the time delay in $Y(s)$.\n\nWe focus on the term with the exponential $e^{-3s}$ (which corresponds to the step function in the time domain) and identify the rational function part:\n\n$$\nF(s) = \\dfrac{1}{s^2(s+4)}\n$$\n\nWe'll solve this in three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Partial fraction decomposition\n\nSince the denominator contains a linear factor $(s+4)$ and a repeated linear factor $s^2$, the decomposition takes the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{s^2(s+4)} = \\dfrac{A}{s} + \\dfrac{B}{s^2} + \\dfrac{C}{s+4}\n$$\n\nTo find the constants, we clear the denominators:\n\n$$\n1 = As(s+4) + B(s+4) + Cs^2\n$$\n\nNow, we find the constants $A, B$, and $C$.\n\n- Setting $s=0$, we have\n$1 = A(0) + B(4) + C(0) \\implies B = \\dfrac{1}{4}$.\n- Setting $s=-4$, we have\n$1 = A(0) + B(0) + C(16) \\implies C = \\dfrac{1}{16}$.\n- Comparing the coefficients of $s^2$, we have\n$0 = A + C \\implies A = -C = -\\dfrac{1}{16}$.\n\nThus, we can rewrite $F(s)$ as\n\n$$\nF(s) = -\\dfrac{1}{16s} + \\dfrac{1}{4s^2} + \\dfrac{1}{16(s+4)}\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Finding the Inverse of the base function\n\nWe find the inverse transform\n\n$$\nf(t) = \\mathcal L^{-1}\\{F(s)\\}\n$$\n\nusing standard transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(t) &= L^{-1}{-\\frac{1}{16s} + \\frac{1}{4s^{2}} + (1)/(16(s + 4))} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{16} + \\frac{t}{4} + \\frac{1}{16}e^{-4t}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Applying the time-shifting property\n\nFinally, we address the $e^{-3s}$ term. By the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\ne^{-as}\\cdot F(s) = \\mathcal L\\{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a)\\}\n$$\n\nTaking the inverse Laplace transform gives\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L^{-1} \\{e^{-as}F(s) \\} = u(t-a) \\cdot f(t-a)\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=3$. Therefore, we shift $f(t)$ by replacing every instance of $t$ with $(t-3){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu(t - 3)f(t - 3) &= u(t - 3)[- \\frac{1}{16} + \\frac{t - 3}{4} + \\frac{1}{16}e^{-4(t - 3)}] \\\\\n&= u(t - 3)(\\frac{1}{16}e^{-4(t - 3)} + \\frac{4t - 13}{16})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing this to the second term of the form given in the problem statement\n\n$$\ny(t) = -2e^{-4t} + u(t-3)\\cdot g(t)\n$$\n\nwe identify $g(t)$ as\n\n$$\ng(t) = \\dfrac{1}{16}e^{-4(t-3)} + \\dfrac{4t-13}{16}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186228630 17592186228631 17592186228632 17592186228633 17592186228634] "immutable_fields" [[17592186228627 :answer-field/id] [17592186228627 :answer-field/key] [17592186228627 :answer-field/type] [17592186228629 :answer-field/correct] [17592186228629 :answer-field/id] [17592186228629 :answer-field/key] [17592186228629 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
